1
00:00:04,890 --> 00:00:21,766
PatientFlow: project walkthrough
Plan outpatient doctor capacity. Compare waiting times on the same patients.

2
00:00:22,703 --> 00:00:40,610
1. Set up the clinic
Change settings first. Run comparison applies them; filters do not rerun the model.

3
00:00:41,547 --> 00:00:59,078
2. Read the recommendation
Synthetic demo: 102 patients. Two doctors compared with four recommended doctors.

4
00:01:00,344 --> 00:01:18,455
3. Compare patient waiting
Mean wait: 80.80 to 2.31 minutes. This is a synthetic simulation, not a clinical claim.

5
00:01:22,953 --> 00:01:40,374
4. Try a busier clinic
Busy clinic loads 18 arrivals/hour. Results change only after Run comparison.

6
00:01:44,594 --> 00:01:59,125
5. Return to the reproducible example
Load demo restores the original settings and seed; run it to reproduce the results.

7
00:02:00,375 --> 00:02:18,521
6. Find the queue bottleneck
Queue charts count waiting patients, not patients already in consultation.

8
00:02:20,531 --> 00:02:39,172
7. Check doctor utilization
Use the same arrival window for a fair comparison; full-session metrics include overtime.

9
00:02:41,437 --> 00:02:58,693
8. Inspect individual patients
Search an ID, switch staffing plans, or show only patients who exceeded the target.

10
00:03:01,531 --> 00:03:18,871
9. Export the comparison
Filtered rows, full paired comparison, and input cohort have separate download buttons.

11
00:03:22,875 --> 00:03:42,955
10. Use the real-source hybrid mode
6,637 Hangu duration estimates + synthetic arrivals. Not a real hospital queue replay.

12
00:03:44,297 --> 00:04:04,477
11. Explain the two techniques
Queueing theory estimates capacity. Discrete-event simulation tests a finite cohort.

13
00:04:05,531 --> 00:04:27,441
Project summary
Local Python + Streamlit. Two OR techniques. Transparent data and reproducible comparisons.
